Here's a complete example. I'll use a hypothetical "Todo API" as the REST service being wrapped.

---

### `TodoApiTools.cs` — Tool class with constructor-injected HttpClient

```csharp
using System.ComponentModel;
using System.Text;
using System.Text.Json;
using ModelContextProtocol.Server;

[McpServerToolType]
public class TodoApiTools(HttpClient httpClient, ILogger<TodoApiTools> logger)
{
    [McpServerTool, Description("List all todo items from the API.")]
    public async Task<string> ListTodos(CancellationToken cancellationToken = default)
    {
        logger.LogInformation("Listing all todos");
        return await httpClient.GetStringAsync("/todos", cancellationToken);
    }

    [McpServerTool, Description("Get a single todo item by its numeric ID.")]
    public async Task<string> GetTodo(
        [Description("The numeric ID of the todo item")] int id,
        CancellationToken cancellationToken = default)
    {
        logger.LogInformation("Fetching todo {Id}", id);
        return await httpClient.GetStringAsync($"/todos/{id}", cancellationToken);
    }

    [McpServerTool(Destructive = false, ReadOnly = false),
     Description("Create a new todo item with a title and completion status.")]
    public async Task<string> CreateTodo(
        [Description("Title of the new todo")] string title,
        [Description("Whether the todo is already completed")] bool completed = false,
        CancellationToken cancellationToken = default)
    {
        var payload = JsonSerializer.Serialize(new { title, completed });
        var content = new StringContent(payload, Encoding.UTF8, "application/json");

        var response = await httpClient.PostAsync("/todos", content, cancellationToken);
        response.EnsureSuccessStatusCode();
        return await response.Content.ReadAsStringAsync(cancellationToken);
    }
}
```

### `Program.cs` — Stdio transport with DI

```csharp
using Microsoft.Extensions.DependencyInjection;
using Microsoft.Extensions.Hosting;
using Microsoft.Extensions.Logging;
using ModelContextProtocol.Server;

var builder = Host.CreateApplicationBuilder(args);

// CRITICAL: route all logging to stderr so stdout stays clean for JSON-RPC
builder.Logging.AddConsole(options =>
    options.LogToStandardErrorThreshold = LogLevel.Trace);

// Register HttpClient with a base address for the API
builder.Services.AddHttpClient<TodoApiTools>(client =>
{
    client.BaseAddress = new Uri("https://jsonplaceholder.typicode.com");
});

builder.Services.AddMcpServer()
    .WithStdioServerTransport()
    .WithToolsFromAssembly();

await builder.Build().RunAsync();
```

---

### How DI works for MCP tools

The SDK has **two injection styles**:

| Style | Class type | How services arrive |
|---|---|---|
| **Constructor injection** | Non-static class | Services in the constructor (like `HttpClient`, `ILogger<T>`) are resolved once when the tool class is instantiated |
| **Method parameter injection** | Static class | DI services appear as method parameters alongside user-facing params |

**Key rule:** The SDK automatically distinguishes DI services from tool parameters. Types registered in the DI container (`HttpClient`, `ILogger<T>`, `CancellationToken`, `IMcpServer`, `IProgress<>`) are **injected silently** — they never appear in the tool's JSON schema that the LLM sees. Only parameters with `[Description]` are exposed to the LLM.

In the example above, `AddHttpClient<TodoApiTools>()` registers a **typed `HttpClient`** with the configured base address. When the SDK instantiates `TodoApiTools` to handle a tool call, the DI container provides the `HttpClient` and `ILogger<TodoApiTools>` via the primary constructor — no manual wiring needed.
